<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Chavín</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 200 BCE (approx.) · capital Chavín de Huántar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A highland religious centre in Peru whose temple, feline-serpent art and cult spread across much of the central Andes, forming one of the first shared cultural horizons of South America.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Chavín de Huántar, at the meeting of the Mosna and Huachecsa rivers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ancash-region">Ancash</a> highlands, was built from around 900 BCE as a temple complex on a route between the coast and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> basin. Its Old Temple, a U-shaped stone platform honeycombed with galleries, grew over centuries into a larger New Temple.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chavín appears to have been a centre of pilgrimage and oracle rather than a conquering state, run by a priestly elite. Inside the Old Temple stands the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanzon">Lanzón</a>, a carved granite monolith of a fanged deity. Priests used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-pedro-cactus">San Pedro cactus</a> and other hallucinogens, and the galleries were designed to channel water and sound. Its art of jaguars, caymans, eagles and snakes appears on textiles, pottery and goldwork far beyond the site, suggesting a widely shared cult.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The influence of Chavín faded between about 500 and 200 BCE as regional cultures such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/paracas">Paracas</a> and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/moche">Moche</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nazca">Nazca</a> developed their own styles. The site was damaged by later occupation and landslides. It was inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1985.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chavin.jpg" alt="Chavín" width="320" height="479"><figcaption>Image: QQuantum · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ChavinMap.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 200 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chavín de Huántar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chavín cult</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chavín de Huántar, Chavín culture</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-550&amp;focus=chavin" aria-label="Chavín on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2551 2536 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chavín lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2871 2792h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2871 2792h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chavín de Huántar. <a href="/atlas?year=-550&amp;focus=chavin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

